WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:24.170 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Laughter]  so depending on how much time all of you  spend on YouTube this may or may not be  the first time you're meeting Micah but  what most people don't know is that  isn't just a blank piece of paper it's  actually one of the many rejection  letters Micah's dad received from  potential employers and although Micah  can't walk or talk or even think really  doesn't mean we don't have anything to  learn from him in fact I think his  ability to express pure joy in a moment  that is otherwise quite tragic perfectly  captures what I want to talk about today  that it's time we start taking left or  seriously and I'm not just talking about  laughter as a distraction or a feel-good  activity or a form of entertainment even  though it is all of those things I'm  talking about laughter as a powerful  tool that individuals and organizations  can use to boost happiness creativity  and productivity that's the average  number of times someone Micah's age  laughs per day in adults over the age of  35 we are in the midst of a laughter  drought and nowhere is this more  apparent than in the world of business  although we are somewhat spoiled here in  Silicon Valley the reality of corporate  America is still very much that of rows  and rows of off-white cubicles silent  except for the mindless clacking of  typing or the sputtering of an espresso  machine if we're lucky we are more tired  more stress more miserable than ever  before half of us at any given time are  unhappy with our current jobs and this  isn't just a touchy-feely issue our  disengagement costs the US economy  three hundred and fifty billion dollars  in lost productivity something happens  to us when we start working we start  seeing work as this thing that depletes  our energy and our happiness that we  then need to replenish on the weekends  or during vacation but even in my very  very young career I've seen the power of  turning this thinking on its head when  I'm in a meeting or a brainstorming  session or trying to survive a group  project nightmare and people are  laughing and enjoying themselves and  losing track of time and still producing  good work there's no better feeling and  no greater validation that I'm doing  exactly what I'm supposed to be doing  now it's foolish to think that any one  approach or solution is going to  re-engage the entire workforce and close  that 350 billion dollar gap but I also  think there are small steps we can take  as individuals and teams to begin  chipping away at the problem and as  someone who measures the success of a  day and in many ways the success of my  life in laughs laughter is for me a  natural place to start think about the  last time you had a really good laugh  and I mean a real laugh not a giggle or  a chuckle or those times you say that  was funny but didn't really mean  anything I'm talking about a laugh that  made you cry that made the muscles in  your stomach hurt where for a few  moments you lost complete control of  your body and your mind was completely  clear didn't it feel so good  clearly she she believes that laughter  has a profound impact in fact on our  brains and our bodies it causes the  release of endorphins our natural  painkillers it lowers stress hormone  levels lowers blood pressure relieves  muscle tension and in some cases even  stimulates white blood cell activity a  study here at Stanford also found that  30 seconds of deep belly laughter is  equivalent to 10 minutes of strenuous  rowing when it comes to its impact on  our heart rate so if like me you've ever  wondered if you can scope the six-pack  while I don't know watching reruns of  Seinfeld in your underwear all weekend  if the answer is yes yes you can but  beyond its impact on our bloodstream in  our brain stem laughter impacts how we  think feel and behave  that should make anyone at work take  note another study out of Wharton found  that individuals primed to laugh before  a complex task exhibit not only more  flexible creative decision-making but  also greater analytical precision  laughter truly engages the whole brain  and even when this laughter is forced  when the people were literally forced to  recreate the muscle movements and breath  patterns associated with laughter they  became more optimistic had greater  perceptions of self-efficacy and felt  more connection to their social groups  and these effects remain 90 days after  the forced laughter occurs  it seems so counterintuitive I know but  laughter is in many ways productivity is  greatest ally it clears the mind and  improves focus it relieves boredom and  reduces the need for us to seek constant  distraction entertainment out of work it  reduces tension and facilitates social  connection these are precisely the  qualities that organizations should be  cultivating in their people yet these  same organizations find themselves  deeply afraid to laugh and that's  because lurking in those off-white  cubicles are the enemies of productivity  their song is waiting to be played and  chats waiting to be had and photo is  waiting to be filtered their blogs and  tumblers of Hillary Clinton texting and  Claire Danes crying waiting to be  scrolled through their videos of  sleeping puppies and kitties waiting to  be watched but what if we stripped  ourselves of this fear what if every  week we challenged every employee to  find the stupidest random thing on the  internet and send it out to the entire  team what if like Zappos we encourage  our customer service representatives to  be themselves and to delight those  customers with jokes and stories and  surprises what if like Hulu we gave  every new employee a nickname held  regular taco eating contests and stocked  our offices with custom-built arizuka's  that's near Zuka in case you're  wondering what if like IBM or Yahoo we  didn't number our meeting rooms but  instead ask our colleagues to meet us in  my network or kajagoogoo or MC Hammer or  what if like Southwest Airlines we  allowed our stock symbol to say  something a little bit more about  ourselves as a company I know from any  of you in this room these aren't  groundbreaking and they may even be  played out but for many companies in  this country and all over the world  these would be huge steps forward and  note that incorporating laughter and  humor into the workplace knows no bounds  of industry or size or geography and  notice that it doesn't necessarily mean  turning your office into a circus it's  about doing it in a way that feels right  and authentic to what you and your  companies stand for and finding that  place where laughter and productivity  converge to create magic for some of you  that may mean having silly  putty and slinkys and magnetic poetry  laying around your cubicles for others  it may mean holding regular office  retreats and off sites with your team  for others it may mean rotating funny  quotes in your email signature or  holding meetings where people are only  allowed to communicate in pictures and  gestures whatever it is find it try it  make it a routine make it a tradition  and let laughter in if any one we do I  think we in the communities and  organizations we inhabit will be happier  more creative and more productive when  you leave here today think about what  laughter might look like on your team in  your company in your life how will you  communicate differently spend your time  differently recruit and hire people  differently so that laughter and humor  become an organic part of your culture  and outside of work ask yourself am i  laughing enough am i giving others  around me the freedom to laugh and am i  intentionally seeking out opportunities  to find wonder and humor in my everyday  life the way mica does when he's ripping  apart a stupid piece of paper and when  it does strike when you have that  perfect moment of clarity that comes  with a good laugh please don't be so  quick to forget where you were and who  was there and how good it felt thank you  so much  you  [Music]

